 For Claude-managed bead workers, the canonical `worker` profile is reified through a first-party runtime-native agent wrapper under [`plugins/caco-agent`](./plugins/caco-agent), but managed launch does not borrow that checkout-local directory directly. The daemon materializes a portable Claude plugin root under the spawned agent directory, writes the generated `worker` agent markdown/frontmatter plus shipped hook assets there, and launches Claude with `--setting-sources project,local --plugin-dir <materialized-plugin-root> --agent caco-agent:worker`. That generated runtime package carries the translated lifecycle hooks, MCP wiring, permission and memory settings, runtime constraints such as `disallowedTools`, and colocated agent hook scripts under `agents/`, while the canonical profile remains the source of truth for worker instructions and completion semantics. The explicit setting-source restriction keeps unrelated user-level Claude plugins and slash-command skills out of managed worker sessions. The canonical worker contract also requires concise `caco msg speak` narration at every major action or phase change — claim/start, complex edit, meaningful implementation milestone, queued validation/build start and finish, blockers/decisions, and reintegration/completion results — while avoiding per-file-edit spam. The canonical worker contract now also requires project-chat coordination for broken-on-main test failures: workers should announce ownership with `caco msg broadcast`, watch `caco msg inbox` for overlap, and either fix the failure themselves or coordinate a clear handoff instead of defaulting to `#[ignore]`. For the canonical direct worker contract, successful `caco agent complete` is expected to both reintegrate the work and close the worker's claimed implementation bead via validated mainline-aware closeout; automatic closeout is limited to bead IDs explicitly present in the worker goal or agent record, and coincidentally claimed unrelated beads are skipped with warnings. When a no-ID `caco bd claim` returns `no_ready_beads`, the structured error includes exclusion counts plus bounded sample bead IDs for assigned, blocked, dispatch-queued, operator-action, epic, pr-in-flight, permanent-tracker, and non-generic-ready-like buckets so workers can inspect why open beads were skipped without assuming board inconsistency.
 
