 <p>PR operations invoke <code>gh</code> through the project wrapper, in this order: <code>CACO_GH_COMMAND_OVERRIDE_&lt;PROJECT&gt;</code>, <code>CACO_GH_COMMAND_OVERRIDE</code>, then the first checkout-bootstrap snippet that defines or wraps <code>gh</code>. This keeps fork/upstream PR work on the same identity policy as the checkout.</p>
 <p>Agents do not run <code>gh pr create</code> (or any other direct PR-publishing <code>gh</code> command) themselves to land their branch. PR creation is owned by the first-party lifecycle surfaces — <code>caco agent reintegrate --mode pr_review</code> / <code>pr_auto_merge</code>, <code>caco agent complete</code>, and the manual <code>caco pr</code> product-PR tool — which invoke <code>gh</code> exactly once through the project wrapper above so the project's configured GitHub username, host, and forge remote (from <code>config.yaml</code> / <code>projects[].remotes</code>) are always the identity that publishes the branch and opens the PR. Even though agents work in daemon-local checkouts whose <code>origin</code> is a mirror, the lifecycle path publishes the agent PR branch to, and opens the PR against, the project's configured forge remote rather than the local mirror, so the configured project is respected end to end.</p>
 
+<h2><code>land-pr</code> is status-only unless critical</h2>
+<p>Landing convergence belongs to the configured queue and controller paths, not to an author-agent watch loop. <code>caco agent land-pr --id &lt;agent&gt;</code> therefore does one <code>gh pr view</code>, reports the PR state plus a no-babysit advisory, and returns; it does not sleep, update the branch, or fall back. The historical bounded client-side update/CI/fallback loop is available only with explicit <code>--critical</code> or <code>CACO_LAND_PR_CLIENT_WATCH=1</code>, for a genuinely critical land classified by an operator/controller. <code>CACO_LAND_QUEUE_ENABLED</code> remains the first gate and makes the command defer without contacting the PR, so a client watch can never race the authoritative daemon queue (bd-c339e1).</p>
+
 <h2>Workflow-file PRs and the <code>workflow</code> OAuth scope</h2>
 <p>A PR-backend PR that modifies <code>.github/workflows/**</code> cannot be brought up-to-date through <code>gh pr update-branch</code>: GitHub refuses the OAuth-app token without the <code>workflow</code> scope (<code>refusing to allow an OAuth App to create or update workflow &hellip; without workflow scope</code>). On a busy <code>main</code> under branch-protection strict/up-to-date, a workflow-file <code>pr_auto_merge</code> PR therefore goes behind and cannot be advanced by <code>gh pr update-branch</code> &mdash; and <code>caco agent land-pr</code> hits the same wall, because it shells the same OAuth path (the token can also rotate mid-flight, so the first update-branch calls may succeed and later ones fail).</p>
 <p>The working land path is <strong>re-reintegration over the daemon's SSH git</strong>: <code>caco agent rebase --id &lt;agent&gt;</code> then <code>caco agent reintegrate --id &lt;agent&gt;</code>. The daemon re-pushes the rebased agent branch to the existing PR branch over its SSH/git credentials &mdash; which already have workflow access, since that is how the PR was opened with the <code>.github/workflows/**</code> change &mdash; idempotently reuses the existing PR (bd-ce2015), and re-arms auto-merge, so the PR becomes up-to-date and CI re-runs. The OAuth <code>workflow</code> scope constraint only affects the <code>gh pr update-branch</code> HTTP path, not the daemon's canonical git push.</p>
